// ElimTrivialBlockArgs — a Std→Std pass that deletes the block-args (phis) that CARRY NOTHING:
// a phi whose every incoming value, once its own self-references are set aside, is the SAME
// single value `v`. Such a phi IS `v` — it merely renames it — so every use of it is rewritten
// to `v` and the slot is dropped.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS: a FALSE E5001 is the worst bug this compiler can have (ARCHITECTURE.md,
// register allocator §"the contract"), and this is the second place one came from.
//
// `pruneDeadBlockArgs` deletes the loop-header phis nothing READS. It deliberately left the
// merely REDUNDANT ones alone — a var the loop reads (or that is read after the loop) but never
// WRITES gets a header phi carrying a value that never changes — on the grounds that "it costs
// nothing: biased coloring coalesces it with its incoming value into one register and SSA
// destruction drops the self-move".
//
// That is true of the REGISTER COUNT and false of everything else. The parser once minted one phi
// per mutable var in scope, so every `var` declared before a loop and read after it became a
// loop-carried phi, self-sustaining through the back edge and therefore live across the ENTIRE
// loop.
//
// ⚠ THE PARSER NO LONGER MINTS THOSE EITHER. It carries a phi only for the names the loop's token
// extent ASSIGNS (`Parser.parseWhileStatement`), and a var that is only READ is not among them.
//
// THE PASS STAYS, because that token scan deliberately OVER-approximates: a `var`/`let` declared
// INSIDE the loop that SHADOWS an outer var of the same name matches the `identifier =` shape, so
// the outer var gets a header phi it does not need — φ(preheader: v, back-edge: v) — and this is
// what folds it. Over-approximating is the safe direction (a surplus phi is a coalesced copy; a
// MISSING one is a miscompile), and this pass is what makes that trade cost nothing. The
// paragraphs below are why it must not simply be tolerated instead:
//
// a phi is precisely what the cold-spill splitter may NOT spill:
// `isColdSpillable` requires a value that is not a phi and not edge-passed, because a cold store
// goes in the PREHEADER and a phi's def is the header itself. So those values are pinned in
// registers across a loop that never touches them, and once they outnumber the pool the compiler
// raises E5001 against a program that fits the machine.
//
// The tell is that `let` and `var` compiled DIFFERENTLY. Fifteen idle `let`s across a loop are
// ordinary values: the splitter spills them around it for free and the program builds. The same
// fifteen written `var` — never reassigned, semantically identical — became fifteen phis, could
// not be spilled, and were refused with E5001 on a loop whose real working set was two.
//
// The parser has always PROMISED this collapse ("phis for unmutated vars turn out trivial and
// collapse in the Std-tier phi-elim pass"). That pass was M4b's `eliminatePhis`, retired at M5
// when the register allocator took over SSA destruction. `pruneDeadBlockArgs` restored its DEAD
// half; this is the TRIVIAL half, and with both of them the promise holds again.
//
// SELF-REFERENCES ARE IGNORED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE TRICK. A loop-header phi's back edge passes
// the phi to ITSELF, so its incoming set is `{v, phi}` and a naive "all incoming equal" test sees
// two distinct values and gives up. Discounting the self-reference is what recognises
// `phi = φ(v, phi)` as just `v`.
//
// A phi whose non-self incoming values genuinely DIFFER (a var written to different values in an
// if/else) is NOT trivial and is left exactly where it is — it is a real merge and it needs a
// real register.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// IT IS A WORKLIST, NOT A RE-SWEEP — AND THAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN LINEAR AND QUADRATIC
// ===========================================================================================
//
// Folding one phi can make ANOTHER phi trivial, in two distinct ways:
//
//   1. a CHAIN — the phi's single incoming is itself a phi that is about to fold. Sequential loops
//      make these, and they are as long as the function: an idle `var` read after a run of N loops
//      gets a header phi in every one of them, and loop L's header phi is fed by loop L-1's EXIT
//      phi for the same var. That is a fold chain of length ~2N.
//   2. a MERGE THAT COLLAPSES — `p = φ(x, y)` with `x ≠ y` is a real merge and not trivial, until
//      `x` folds to `y` and it becomes `φ(y, y)`.
//
// This pass used to answer both by RE-SWEEPING the whole function until a sweep changed nothing.
// Each sweep is linear, but the SWEEP COUNT was never bounded, and on a chain it is the length of
// the chain: `collectReadyArgs` deliberately deferred any phi whose fold TARGET was also folding
// this sweep, so a sweep peeled exactly ONE link off the chain. N sweeps × O(function) each is
// O(N × function) — QUADRATIC, and measured at x3.95 allocations / x4.85 bytes / x4.23 time per
// DOUBLING on a function of N sequential loops holding one idle var. The pass folded a LINEAR
// number of phis (2N of them) and did QUADRATIC work to find them, which is the tell.
//
// A worklist answers both causes exactly and once. When a phi folds, the ONLY phis whose answer
// can change are the ones that READ it, and `FeedTriggers` names them in O(1). So each phi is
// re-examined only when one of its own operands actually moved, never because something unrelated
// on the far side of the function did.
//
// CHAINS ARE RESOLVED BY UNION-FIND, WITH PATH COMPRESSION, so a chain of length N is flattened
// the first time it is walked instead of once per sweep. Folding `a → b` where `b` later folds to
// `v` needs no chain resolution and no deferral: `a`'s root simply BECOMES `v`, and the
// substitution column handed to the rewrite is the fully-compressed forest. That is also why the
// old "defer a phi whose target is also folding" rule is gone — it existed only to stop a
// one-substitution-at-a-time rewrite from leaving a dangling reference to a slot it was about to
// delete, and a root is by construction not a slot that is about to be deleted.
//
// AND THE TRIGGER INDEX MUST MIGRATE WITH THE CHAIN, WHICH IS THE WHOLE SUBTLETY OF THE WORKLIST.
// When `f` folds to `h`, every phi that read `f` now reads `h` — so when `h` ITSELF folds, those
// same phis must be re-asked AGAIN. An index keyed on the ORIGINAL incoming value files them under
// `f` and never under `h`, so `h`'s fold never reaches them and a phi that has BECOME trivial is
// left standing. `FeedTriggers` therefore re-files each operand slot onto the fold's new root as
// the fold happens, which is the side-index equivalent of what a use-list-based SSA construction
// gets for free by physically rewriting the operand.

// What each phi carries, accumulated across the edges that feed it: has a non-self value arrived
// yet, what was it, and did a LATER one disagree. THE TRIVIALITY RULE LIVES HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE —
// it is asked in two different iterations (once over every edge slot in the function, and again over
// one phi's feeders each time one of them folds) and a second copy of "what makes a phi trivial"
// would be a second chance to disagree about it.
//
// `differs` is what makes a REAL merge — a var written to different values in an if/else — survive
// untouched.
//
// DENSE COLUMNS, indexed by ValueId, NOT a Map: block-arg ids are function-local and dense, which is
// the same discipline the register allocator's columns follow.
type CarriedValues
	var seen as BoolArray
	var only as ValueIdArray
	var differs as BoolArray

	static function create(bound ValueId) returns CarriedValues
		var seen = BoolArray.create()
		var only = ValueIdArray.create()
		var differs = BoolArray.create()
		seen.resize(bound)
		only.resize(bound)
		differs.resize(bound)
		return Self{seen: seen, only: only, differs: differs}
	end 'create'

	// Fold one incoming value into `arg`'s accumulator.
	//
	// A SELF-REFERENCE IS DISCOUNTED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE TRICK: a loop-header phi's back edge
	// passes the phi to ITSELF, so its incoming set is `{v, phi}` and a naive "all incoming equal"
	// test sees two distinct values and gives up. `phi = φ(v, phi)` is just `v`. Once a chain has
	// folded, an incoming that RESOLVED back to this phi is the same thing and is discounted too.
	function note(arg ValueId, incoming ValueId)
		if incoming == arg 'selfReference'
			return
		end 'selfReference'

		let alreadySeen = try self.seen.get(arg) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: block-arg {arg} is outside the block-arg id space")
		if not alreadySeen 'first'
			try self.seen.set(arg, value: true) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: seen.set({arg}) OOB")
			try self.only.set(arg, value: incoming) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: only.set({arg}) OOB")
			return
		end 'first'

		let carried = try self.only.get(arg) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: only.get({arg}) OOB")
		if incoming != carried 'realMerge'
			try self.differs.set(arg, value: true) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: differs.set({arg}) OOB")
		end 'realMerge'
	end 'note'

	// Forget everything accumulated for `arg`, so its feeders can be re-read through the fold chain.
	function forget(arg ValueId)
		try self.seen.set(arg, value: false) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: seen.set({arg}) OOB")
		try self.differs.set(arg, value: false) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: differs.set({arg}) OOB")
	end 'forget'

	// Trivial iff exactly one distinct non-self value ever arrived. A phi with NO non-self incoming
	// at all (an unreachable block, or a phi fed only by itself) is NOT trivial: there is no value
	// it could BE.
	function isTrivial(arg ValueId) returns bool
		let hasIncoming = try self.seen.get(arg) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: seen.get({arg}) OOB")
		let isRealMerge = try self.differs.get(arg) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: differs.get({arg}) OOB")
		return hasIncoming and not isRealMerge
	end 'isTrivial'

	// The single value a trivial `arg` carries.
	function targetOf(arg ValueId) returns ValueId
		return try self.only.get(arg) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: only.get({arg}) OOB")
	end 'targetOf'
end 'CarriedValues'

// WHICH PHI-OPERAND SLOTS A VALUE CURRENTLY FEEDS — the index the worklist fires on when a phi
// folds, and the one structure in this pass that MOVES as the folds happen.
//
// The question a fold has to answer is "whose answer can have changed?", and the answer is "every
// phi that reads the value I just folded". Read once, that is a static (incoming → arg) index. But
// a fold RE-POINTS a read: after `f` folds to `h`, a phi that used to read `f` reads `h`, and when
// `h` in turn folds that phi must be re-asked a SECOND time. A static index files it under `f`
// forever, so `h`'s fold never names it — and a phi that has become trivial is left standing, which
// is precisely the thing this pass exists to prevent (see the header: a surviving phi is
// unspillable, and that is where a FALSE E5001 comes from). It is a silent miss, not a crash: the
// IR is correct, merely worse, and the 213-spec corpus does not contain the shape.
//
// So the SLOTS MIGRATE. Each operand slot lives on the list of the value its incoming operand
// currently RESOLVES to. Folding `f` walks f's list, re-asks every reader, and re-files the
// surviving slots onto f's new ROOT — so the next fold in the chain walks them again. This is what
// a use-list-based SSA construction gets for free by physically rewriting the operand; a pass that
// defers its rewrite to the end has to carry the same fact itself.
//
// Two slots are dropped rather than re-filed, and both are dropped FOREVER:
//   • one whose READER has itself folded — a deleted phi has no answer left to change;
//   • one whose new root is NOT a block-arg (an op result, a parameter) — that root can never fold,
//     so nothing will ever fire on it again.
// That is what keeps the migration linear on the fold CHAIN the header describes: every reader in
// the chain folds, so the lists empty as they are walked rather than accumulating.
type FeedTriggers
	// Slot index PLUS ONE, in both columns, so a zero-filled column reads as "feeds nothing" and
	// "end of list" with no sentinel carved out of the slot-index space. Same discipline as
	// `buildBlockIdIndex`, and for the same reason.
	var head as ValueIdArray
	var next as ValueIdArray

	static function create(pairs PhiOperandPairs, bound ValueId) returns FeedTriggers
		var head = ValueIdArray.create()
		var next = ValueIdArray.create()
		head.resize(bound)
		next.resize(pairs.count())

		var triggers = Self{head: head, next: next}

		// A slot whose incoming value is AT OR ABOVE the block-arg id space is fed by an op result
		// or a parameter. That value can never fold, so no fold will ever fire on it and the slot
		// needs no list — and it must be kept out of a column whose key space spans exactly the
		// block-args.
		for i in 0 upto pairs.count() 'eachSlot'
			let incoming = pairs.incomingAt(i)
			if incoming >= bound 'neverFolds'
				continue
			end 'neverFolds'

			triggers.attach(incoming, slot: i)
		end 'eachSlot'

		return triggers
	end 'create'

	// File `slot` under `v`, at the head of its list.
	function attach(v ValueId, slot ValueId)
		let first = try self.head.get(v) otherwise panic("FeedTriggers: head.get({v}) is outside the block-arg id space")
		try self.next.set(slot, value: first) otherwise panic("FeedTriggers: next.set({slot}) OOB")
		try self.head.set(v, value: slot + 1) otherwise panic("FeedTriggers: head.set({v}) OOB")
	end 'attach'

	function headOf(v ValueId) returns ValueId
		return try self.head.get(v) otherwise panic("FeedTriggers: head.get({v}) is outside the block-arg id space")
	end 'headOf'

	function nextOf(slot ValueId) returns ValueId
		return try self.next.get(slot) otherwise panic("FeedTriggers: next.get({slot}) OOB")
	end 'nextOf'

	// Detach the whole list in one move. The caller has taken its head and is walking it; leaving
	// the slots ALSO reachable from `v` would re-file each of them twice the next time anything
	// asked what `v` feeds.
	function detach(v ValueId)
		try self.head.set(v, value: 0) otherwise panic("FeedTriggers: head.set({v}) OOB")
	end 'detach'
end 'FeedTriggers'

// Fold every trivial block-arg in every function, in place.
export function elimTrivialBlockArgs(module StdModule)
	var removed = 0 as ValueId
	// ⭐⭐ **AN INDEX LOOP, NOT `for … in`, AND THE REASON IS A ⚖ USER RULING (2026-08-26). THIS IS THE
	// CANONICAL SITE; TWENTY-FOUR OTHERS POINT HERE.** `for func in module.functions` hands the body a BORROW
	// of the element, and the E3070 SUBJECT of that borrow is the whole `module` record —
	// `Parser.iterationSubjectNameAt` names a chain's BASE, necessarily: a callee handed `module` can reach
	// every field of it. The loop element's borrow is LEXICAL, running to the loop's `end` (exactly what
	// `borrow-liveness:forin-element-borrow-is-lexical` pins), so it is still live when the call below takes
	// `module` mutably. **E3070, and it is correct.**
	//
	// ⚠ **THE REFUSAL IS THE RULING, NOT A DEFECT TO ROUTE AROUND.** shv2 does not retain on element read,
	// so a callee that cleared `module.functions` would leave that borrow pointing at freed memory — measured
	// at P1.8 as the `0x3F` free-poison byte. The runnable oracle COMPILES the `for … in` spelling, because it
	// retains; shv2 refuses it, deliberately, and `specs-shv2/borrow-liveness.md` carries both answers. Do not
	// "fix" this by weakening E3070, the iteration lock or `iterationSubjectNameAt`.
	//
	// ⇒ **THE IDIOM IS `count()` + `get(i)`, AND ONE EXPRESSION SUPPLIES BOTH.** A `get` borrow has ordinary
	// last-use liveness, so it dies at the call it feeds and nothing is live across the write.
	// `SemanticCheck.resolveOverloadedCalls` already carries this same argument one level down, where the
	// ORDER of its match arms is load-bearing for it. The bound is evaluated ONCE into the preheader, which is
	// what every one of these walks means: none resizes the array it is walking, and one that did would be
	// precisely the bug the borrow exists to catch.
	for fnIndex in 0 upto module.functions.count() 'eachFunc'
		removed = removed + elimTrivialBlockArgsInFunc(module, func: try module.functions.get(fnIndex) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: fnIndex {fnIndex} is below module.functions.count()"))
	end 'eachFunc'

	let total = removed
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "elimTrivialBlockArgs: folded {total} trivial block-arg(s) across {module.funcCount()} function(s)")
end 'elimTrivialBlockArgs'

// Fold one function to its fixpoint in a SINGLE pass over the IR. Returns how many block-args it
// folded away.
function elimTrivialBlockArgsInFunc(module StdModule, func IrFunction) returns ValueId
	let bound = blockArgIdBound(module, func: func)
	if bound == 0 'noBlockArgs'
		// A straight-line function has no phis to fold, and no columns to size.
		return 0
	end 'noBlockArgs'

	let refByBlockId = buildBlockIdIndex(module, func: func)
	let pairs = collectPhiOperands(module, func: func, refByBlockId: refByBlockId)

	// ONE pass over every edge slot in the function. Nothing has folded yet, so an incoming value
	// stands for itself and no chain resolution is needed.
	var carried = CarriedValues.create(bound)
	for i in 0 upto pairs.count() 'eachSlot'
		carried.note(pairs.argAt(i), incoming: pairs.incomingAt(i))
	end 'eachSlot'

	// SEED THE WORKLIST, AND BAIL IF IT IS EMPTY — the common case, and worth its own exit.
	//
	// A phi's answer changes only when one of its OWN feeders folds. So if not one phi in the
	// function is trivial with nothing yet folded, not one ever will be: there is no first fold to
	// start the cascade. A function whose every merge is a REAL merge stops here — and that is most
	// of them, including the two largest shapes the scale corpus generates (a chain of `if`s over one
	// accumulator merges a different value on each path, every time). It stops before the feeder
	// index, the user index, the fold chain and the whole-function rewrite are ever allocated, which
	// is what keeps this pass's cost on ordinary code where the old re-sweep had it.
	var queued = BoolArray.create()
	queued.resize(bound)

	var worklist = ValueIdArray.create()
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		for arg in block.blockArgs 'eachArg'
			if carried.isTrivial(arg.valueId) 'trivial'
				enqueue(arg.valueId, worklist: worklist, queued: queued)
			end 'trivial'
		end 'eachArg'
	end 'eachBlock'

	if worklist.count() == 0 'everyMergeIsReal'
		return 0
	end 'everyMergeIsReal'

	// Something WILL fold, so now — and only now — the machinery a fold needs.
	//
	//   • `feeders[arg]` — what is passed to this phi, so it can be re-read through the fold chain.
	//   • `triggers`     — which operand slots does this value currently feed: the question the
	//                      worklist asks after every fold, and the one a re-sweep answered by
	//                      re-reading the FUNCTION.
	//   • `parent`       — the fold chain, as a union-find column seeded with the identity.
	let feeders = buildCsr(pairs.args, to: pairs.incoming, nodeCount: bound as CsrIndex)
	var triggers = FeedTriggers.create(pairs, bound: bound)

	var parent = ValueIdArray.create()
	parent.resize(bound)
	for v in 0 upto bound 'identity'
		try parent.set(v, value: v) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: parent.set({v}) OOB against a column of {bound}")
	end 'identity'

	var folded = BoolArray.create()
	folded.resize(bound)

	while worklist.count() > 0 'settle'
		let arg = try worklist.pop() otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: pop from a non-empty worklist")
		try queued.set(arg, value: false) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: queued.set({arg}) OOB")

		if isFolded(folded, v: arg) 'alreadyGone'
			continue
		end 'alreadyGone'
		if not carried.isTrivial(arg) 'realMerge'
			continue
		end 'realMerge'

		try folded.set(arg, value: true) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: folded.set({arg}) OOB")
		try parent.set(arg, value: carried.targetOf(arg)) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: parent.set({arg}) OOB")

		relayFold(triggers, pairs: pairs, carried: carried, feeders: feeders, parent: parent, folded: folded, worklist: worklist, queued: queued, arg: arg, bound: bound)
	end 'settle'

	// The compressed forest IS the substitution: after this, `parent[v]` is the value `v` ultimately
	// became, and `v` itself where nothing changed. No second column, and no chains left in it.
	compressAll(parent, bound: bound)
	substituteValuesInFunc(module, func: func, subst: parent)

	// `folded` is this pass's working column — a fold is an EVENT, and the settle loop asks "has this
	// one already gone?". The shared dropper asks the OPPOSITE question, "does this one survive?", and
	// inverting it here, once, is what lets both block-arg passes hand `dropBlockArgs` the same kind of
	// column. A polarity flag on the dropper would be one more thing the two passes could disagree
	// about, and the slot alignment it maintains is the one thing that cannot survive a disagreement.
	var kept = flagColumn(bound)
	for v in 0 upto bound 'survivors'
		if not isFolded(folded, v: v) 'survives'
			setFlag(kept, v: v)
		end 'survives'
	end 'survivors'

	return dropBlockArgs(module, func: func, refByBlockId: refByBlockId, kept: kept)
end 'elimTrivialBlockArgsInFunc'

// `arg` has just folded. Re-ask every phi that READS it — those, and only those, can have changed
// their answer, and re-reading exactly them is what a sweep spent a whole pass over the IR to
// discover — and then RE-FILE their operand slots onto `arg`'s new root, so the NEXT fold in the
// chain re-asks them too. Dropping the re-file is what leaves a trivial phi standing; see the
// `FeedTriggers` header.
function relayFold(triggers FeedTriggers, pairs PhiOperandPairs, carried CarriedValues, feeders CsrGraph, parent ValueIdArray, folded BoolArray, worklist ValueIdArray, queued BoolArray, arg ValueId, bound ValueId)
	// The value `arg` HAS BECOME, and therefore the value its readers now read. It is a root in one
	// step, not a walk: a fold that moves the root of anything `arg` reads re-reads `arg` first —
	// that is what the migrating trigger list guarantees — so `carried.targetOf` was already a root
	// when the caller stored it. `findRoot` is here to STATE that invariant, and it is also what
	// makes the forest provably ACYCLIC: a fold only ever points a block-arg at a current ROOT.
	let root = findRoot(parent, v: arg, bound: bound)

	var slot = triggers.headOf(arg)
	triggers.detach(arg)

	while slot > 0 'eachReader'
		let thisSlot = slot - 1
		let following = triggers.nextOf(thisSlot)
		let reader = pairs.argAt(thisSlot)

		// A reader that has already folded has no answer left to change, and a slot whose root can
		// never fold can never fire again. Either way the slot is dropped for good, and that is what
		// stops the migrating lists from accumulating along a chain.
		if not isFolded(folded, v: reader) 'live'
			reread(carried, feeders: feeders, parent: parent, arg: reader, bound: bound)
			enqueue(reader, worklist: worklist, queued: queued)

			if root < bound 'rootCanStillFold'
				triggers.attach(root, slot: thisSlot)
			end 'rootCanStillFold'
		end 'live'

		slot = following
	end 'eachReader'
end 'relayFold'

// Re-read `arg`'s feeders through the fold chain, replacing whatever was accumulated for it. Called
// when one of its feeders has just folded — which is the only event that can change its answer.
function reread(carried CarriedValues, feeders CsrGraph, parent ValueIdArray, arg ValueId, bound ValueId)
	carried.forget(arg)
	for k in 0 upto feeders.degree(arg as CsrIndex) 'eachFeeder'
		carried.note(arg, incoming: findRoot(parent, v: feeders.neighbour(arg as CsrIndex, k: k) as ValueId, bound: bound))
	end 'eachFeeder'
end 'reread'

// Push `arg` unless it is already waiting. Without the `queued` column a phi fed by many edges
// would be pushed once per edge, and the worklist — not the work — would be what grew.
function enqueue(arg ValueId, worklist ValueIdArray, queued BoolArray)
	let waiting = try queued.get(arg) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: queued.get({arg}) OOB")
	if waiting 'alreadyWaiting'
		return
	end 'alreadyWaiting'

	try queued.set(arg, value: true) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: queued.set({arg}) OOB")
	worklist.push(arg)
end 'enqueue'

// The value `v` ultimately becomes: follow the fold chain to the value that is not itself folded.
//
// PATH-COMPRESSED — every link on the path is repointed straight at the root — so a chain of length
// N is flattened the first time it is walked and every later query on it is O(1). That is what keeps
// the pass linear when the parser hands it a fold chain as long as the function, which a run of
// sequential loops does (see the header).
//
// A value at or above `bound` is not a block-arg, cannot fold, and is its own root. That is the
// chain's terminator, and it is why the column need only span the block-arg id space.
function findRoot(parent ValueIdArray, v ValueId, bound ValueId) returns ValueId
	if v >= bound 'notABlockArg'
		return v
	end 'notABlockArg'

	var root = v
	while root < bound 'toRoot'
		let next = try parent.get(root) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: parent.get({root}) OOB against a column of {bound}")
		if next == root 'isRoot'
			break
		end 'isRoot'
		root = next
	end 'toRoot'

	var cur = v
	while cur < bound 'compress'
		let next = try parent.get(cur) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: parent.get({cur}) OOB against a column of {bound}")
		if next == cur 'atRoot'
			break
		end 'atRoot'
		try parent.set(cur, value: root) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: parent.set({cur}) OOB")
		cur = next
	end 'compress'

	return root
end 'findRoot'

// Flatten the whole forest in place, so `parent` becomes a plain substitution column: one `findRoot`
// per entry, each of which path-compresses what it walks. `substituteValuesInFunc` can then read it as
// a lookup with no chains left in it — and `remappedValue` reads any value at or above `bound` as
// itself, which is exactly right for the op results and parameters that never fold.
function compressAll(parent ValueIdArray, bound ValueId)
	for v in 0 upto bound 'each'
		_ = findRoot(parent, v: v, bound: bound)
	end 'each'
end 'compressAll'

function isFolded(folded BoolArray, v ValueId) returns bool
	return try folded.get(v) otherwise panic("elimTrivialBlockArgs: folded.get({v}) is outside the block-arg id space")
end 'isFolded'
